WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.396 --> 00:00:05.770
PRESENTER 1: My
SuperUROP project

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:08.620
is about making a
program run faster.

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:11.136
PRESENTER 2: Developing the
iOS version of App Inventor

00:00:11.136 --> 00:00:13.010
in order to democratize
cross-platform mobile

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:14.131
development.

00:00:14.131 --> 00:00:16.630
PRESENTER 3: Routing of drones
in communications-constrained

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:17.470
environments.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.990
PRESENTER 4: Introduce
economic and political factors

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:22.300
into a machine learning model.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.460
PRESENTER 5: Helping
teachers give better feedback

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:25.989
on student's code.

00:00:25.989 --> 00:00:28.030
PRESENTER 6: This project
was a new field for me.

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:29.950
It involved computer
vision and robotics.

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:31.450
PRESENTER 7: We did
a clinical trial

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.274
with 3 donors and 11 patients.

00:00:33.274 --> 00:00:34.690
PRESENTER 8: I got
the opportunity

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:36.640
to try out one of
these algorithms

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.890
at the Boston Symphony
Orchestra a couple of weeks

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:40.715
ago, with very
promising results.

00:00:40.715 --> 00:00:42.340
PRESENTER 9: Being
part of this program

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.940
has forced me to better
summarize and communicate

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:46.856
ideas.

00:00:46.856 --> 00:00:48.980
PRESENTER 10: Working
one-on-one with my supervisor

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.165
was extremely useful.

00:00:51.165 --> 00:00:53.290
PRESENTER 2: I want to
mobile development as a job,

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:55.360
and so it gave me a very
big full-year project

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:57.100
to work on that.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.590
PRESENTER 1: It gives you some
guidance and an opportunity

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:01.330
to really know
how to communicate

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.414
your work in a professional
and academic setting.

00:01:04.414 --> 00:01:05.830
PRESENTER 2: It
gave me the skills

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:07.621
when it came to writing
an actual proposal,

00:01:07.621 --> 00:01:09.279
writing a research paper.

00:01:09.279 --> 00:01:11.320
PRESENTER 5: It allowed
me to implement a product

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.130
and add features to
something that existed,

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.460
which is something that
I really love to do.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.680
PRESENTER 9: It forces
you to take a problem

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.430
and kind of break it down into
its fundamental root causes.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:24.130
That's a great skill to have.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

